  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild daytime temperatures through Thursday.\n\n-  Patchy valley fog around sunrise each morning.\n\n-  Significant weather change coming Friday into the weekend.\n\nA broad ridge of high pressure will continue to bring mild daytime\ntemperatures across the Northern Rockies through Thursday. Stable\nconditions under the ridge are promoting the development of daily valley\ninversions and trapped cold air, with patchy dense fog. Afternoon\ntemperatures will continue to run 10 to 15 degrees above normal for this\ntime of year. Forecast models suggest a few isolated showers developing\nover Lemhi County this afternoon, then over southwest and west-central\nMontana tomorrow afternoon and evening.\n\nExpect a significant change as a low pressure system and cold front track\nacross the region Thursday night into Friday. The front will bring wind\ngusts around 20 mph in Idaho and 20 to 35 mph across most of western\nMontana on Friday. Moisture with this system will move north from the Great\nBasin, with precipitation focusing along and south of the I-90 corridor and\nalong the Continental Divide in northwest Montana. Most of the\nprecipitation will fall as rain ahead of the cold front, limiting the\npotential for winter weather impacts. Snow levels will fall to around 5,000\nfeet Friday and lower over the weekend as shower activity begins to\ndecrease. Therefore, forecast snow amounts will be generally under\nan inch across the region and above 5,000 feet. The exception is \nthe higher elevations of the Flathead National Forest and Glacier \nNational Park, which will likely pick up several inches of snow \nthis weekend.\n\nThere remains considerable uncertainty in the extended forecast as the\nmajority of long-range forecast models have different opinions on the\noverall pattern early next week. Twenty percent of the models show another\nridge of high pressure returning to the region, with the other models\nsuggesting a southwest flow or a cold Canadian trough. Stay tuned.",
